{\pard\sa120 Dear Raj,\par}
{\pard\sa120 Thank you for seeing Mr. van Wyk, a 94-year-old man who is well known along the waterfront here as the last surviving Morehead City harbor pilot of his generation. He lives at Windward Commons assisted living and remains sharp and independent.\par}
{\pard\sa120 He has a dual-chamber pacemaker placed in 2017 for complete heart block (generator serial PJK418873H). On February 1 he had a brief syncopal episode at breakfast. Staff found him pale but alert within a minute; he refused transport. I saw him two days later. ECG showed a paced rhythm at 60. Our in-office device check suggests the battery is near elective replacement, and it logged eleven episodes of atrial fibrillation since October, the longest about six hours.\par}
{\pard\sa120 Other history: chronic kidney disease, stage 3; prostate cancer treated with radiation in 2009; an echocardiogram last spring showed an ejection fraction of 45%. Medications: apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily (started by me on the 3rd; CHA2DS2-VASc of 4), metoprolol succinate 25 mg, tamsulosin, vitamin D. No known drug allergies.\par}
{\pard\sa120 I would be grateful for your opinion on generator replacement, and on whether the syncope is device-related. Neil has told me plainly that he does not want resuscitation but does want the pacemaker kept working. His daughter and health care agent, Annika van Wyk-Bauer, will bring him; please arrange the visit through her at (919) 555-0178 or annika.vwb@mailhaven.example. He has Medicare Part B and a supplement, so no authorization is needed.\par}
{\pard\sa120 With thanks and kind regards,\par}
